1. e4 e6 2. d4 a6 3. Nf3 b5 4. Bd3 c5 5. c3 Bb7 6. O-O Nf6 7. Qe2 Be7 ({I had not thought of} 7... Nc6 {at this time.}) 8. a4 {It is not clear that Black holds the balance in these lines. In the game Black chooses a worse reply after which he is positionally lost. However, Bednarski's time trouble and desire to mate at all costs have their say, and eventually it is Black who comes away with the full point!} cxd4 (8... b4 9. e5 Nd5 10. c4 Nc7) (8... bxa4 9. Rxa4 Qc7 (9... O-O 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. e5 Nd5 12. Bxh7+ {looks too strong})) (8... c4 9. Bc2 d5 10. e5 Nfd7) 9. axb5 dxc3 $2 10. bxa6 c2 11. Nc3 Bc8 $1 12. e5 Nd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 14. Nd4 Nc6 15. Nf5 ({After the game Bednarski showed the variation} 15. Nxc6 dxc6 16. Qxc2 Qb6 17. Be3 c5 18. Rfc1 d4 19. Bxd4 cxd4 20. Qxc8+ Rxc8 21. Rxc8+ {with advantage to White. The move he plays is also strong of course.}) 15... Kf8 16. Ra4 h5 17. Bxc2 ({Begins to go astray. Better simply} 17. Nxe7 Qxe7 18. b4 {and} Nxb4 {fails to} 19. Ba3) 17... Bc5 $1 {Counterplay!} 18. Be3 Qb6 19. Nd6 d4 20. Bf4 Bxa6 21. Bd3 Bb7 {One danger removed - the passed a-pawn. Now only the king has to be saved.} 22. Rxa8+ Bxa8 23. Bc4 Nd8 24. Ra1 Bc6 25. Bg5 Bxd6 26. exd6 Ne6 27. Be7+ Kg8 28. Qd2 Rh6 29. b4 Rg6 30. Bf1 d3 {Black goes on the offensive. 31...Nd4 - b3 or f3 are two troublesome threats.} 31. Ra3 Nd4 32. Rxd3 Nf3+ 33. Rxf3 Bxf3 34. Qc3 Qb7 35. g3 Re6 36. Qc7 Re1 37. Qxb7 Bxb7 38. b5 Bf3 39. b6 Be2 {Source: St George's Opening by MJ Basman (Audio Chess)} 0-1

